-- Corrects a real bug in 20260918210000: that migration keyed its
-- UPDATEs off LOCAL Postgres's own book UUIDs, which do NOT match
-- hosted's UUIDs for the same logical book (confirmed directly --
-- e.g. 'Butcher & Blackbird' is 6738693f-... locally but 4d9f7ef2-...
-- on hosted) -- exactly the mistake CLAUDE.md's own "reference via a
-- title subselect, never a raw UUID" rule exists to prevent. Caught
-- immediately by verifying against real hosted after applying (only
-- 166 of 1254 rows had actually updated -- the ones where local and
-- hosted UUIDs happened to coincide), not by assuming success from
-- `db push`'s own report (which shows no error either way, since a
-- WHERE clause matching zero rows isn't a SQL error).
--
-- Re-keyed by (title, author) instead -- confirmed unique across the
-- whole catalog (zero duplicate (title, author) pairs) -- while still
-- pointing at the SAME already-uploaded Storage object (named by the
-- LOCAL book id, which is fine -- that filename is just a string, not
-- a foreign key, and doesn't need to match hosted's own id for that
-- book at all).

